    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To see why the position and momentum uncertainty relation holds, consider a particle described by a wave packet $\\psi(x)$, which is a superposition of plane waves $e^{ikx}$. If a particle has a precise momentum $p = \\hbar k$, its wavefunction is an infinite plane wave extending over all space, meaning the position $x$ is completely undefined with infinite uncertainty. Conversely, if we confine a particle to a precise region (small $\\Delta x$) by forming a sharp wave packet from many different plane waves, the spread of wave numbers $k$ becomes large, resulting in large uncertainty in momentum. This trade-off arises directly from the Fourier transform pair between position space and momentum space, where $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\hbar/2$ is a mathematical lower bound for any square-integrable function that isn't zero. This geometric relationship between the widths of the two transforms in orthogonal space means that information in one domain is fundamentally distributed in the other. Furthermore, while the position-momentum relation is static and involves operators with eigenstates, the energy-time uncertainty principle is distinct because time $t$ is not an operator in standard quantum mechanics but rather a parameter labeling the evolution of the",
      "source": "target",
